      "field_name": "exteroceptive_refl",
      "value": "1",
      "quote": "Besides to drugs enhancing GABA neurotransmission, SPS symptoms can be treated by anti-epileptics[1] such as gabapentin [9]. Pregabalin is an antiepileptic with a chemical structure similar to GABA [10] but it is not a GABA-mimetic nor does it bind to GABA A or GABAB receptors. It is thought to inhibit calcium influx and subsequent release of excitatory neurotransmitters, including glutamate and norepinephrine[10]. As a possible mechanism of action in SPS, pregabalin might have compensated the imbalance between inhibitory and excitatory intracortical circuits. This hypothesis is supported by the prolongation of the GABAB mediated CSP after pregabalin introduction. Interestingly, similarly to osteotendinous reflexes, plantar responses were normalized after 3 months treatment. Extensor plantar response were occasionally reported in SPS [11, 12], possibly reflecting the enhancement of exteroceptive reflexes which is a neurophysiological hallmark of SPS [1].",
